#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184098 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f0567d5-4622-5c34-a448-0bc043fec12f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "9bdbb969-4745-503a-ba4b-fb07529b1b6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9627" :question/problem "![](images/57/57be4d7696363594e9d100ba75a2df1acc9742b8604ead7c9d1176c51571032d.png)\n\nThe function $y = f(x)$ is shown above. Calculate $f(-2) + f(1).$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-2,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(-2)=-2.$\n\n- When $x=1,$ the function is **undefined** because the function has an open circle at this point.\n\nTherefore, since $f(1)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-2)+f(1)$ is undefined too."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184099 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55343d43-c173-5ba8-9a0e-58a76a282b7f" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3ab53f11-138b-5c41-ae90-0158c3fc8a5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9636" :question/problem "What is the graph of $y = f(x),$ where $f(x) = 2x - 3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of $y = 2x - 3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y = 2x - 3.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x = 0$ then $y = 2(0) - 3 = -3,$ which gives the point $(0,-3),$ and\n\n- if $x = 1$ then $y = 2(1) - 3 = -1,$ which gives the point $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs in the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/9f/9f3005c9fac13668b126396f11aa8f3141d6de63d6e88e90d270901a9253fbd1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f0567d5-4622-5c34-a448-0bc043fec12f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "770c4525-28c6-5ebf-b73a-afcf855636a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501688} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca7c832-7633-5f70-977c-619d612db5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501699} {:answer/id #uuid "c5127028-73db-5dac-907b-e57b56d7e94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501858} {:answer/id #uuid "edc9a486-f337-56d4-8c0e-9b9f2836087c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501946} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b254fd-0be4-5a19-9d61-a7d3cbf5d222" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501959}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "770c4525-28c6-5ebf-b73a-afcf855636a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501688} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c2f4be-574d-5a90-9082-145cbb9b7166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "930ee824-1c66-5c5d-8da9-b30bfa636dd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-130517" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f744e2729965bffbf281aee4a2bb2ecbdca635035e5fefca8f992d59d2590a1.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is given by the graph above. Calculate $f(1)+f(4).$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=1,$ we have $y=2.$ So, $f(1)=2.$\n\n- When $x=4,$ we have $y=3.$ So, $f(4)=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1)+f(4)=2+3=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f0567d5-4622-5c34-a448-0bc043fec12f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77a7f61a-dc5a-542a-8df3-cadc1317aa06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501690} {:answer/id #uuid "83413367-58be-5089-bb29-605318b5f7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501718} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e0269f-d14a-56dc-8bd8-b5d01d7255d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501796} {:answer/id #uuid "aac89784-adf4-5b95-b933-f9d7b9856ca3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501802} {:answer/id #uuid "c9e0d5ed-7875-597c-99c6-46920be65b78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77a7f61a-dc5a-542a-8df3-cadc1317aa06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501690} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2edd12c-7bfd-53fc-9971-080daa8a2888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13822b6c-654c-513f-8912-18bafd642a51" :question/math-academy-id "q-130553" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0fa5339a6017befb3ac178aa474b6455a5392f79738d2faa3f8861a987c5488b.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Calculate $f(-3)+f(3).$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-3,$ the function is undefined.\n\n- When $x=3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(3)=-2.$\n\nTherefore, since $f(-3)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-3)+f(3)$ is undefined too."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184201 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f0567d5-4622-5c34-a448-0bc043fec12f" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function Given Graphically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00206c99-c52e-5e11-a583-8f426daa9681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501462} {:answer/id #uuid "64f3340a-f6df-5ada-a6b8-e70d6f0f39ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501652} {:answer/id #uuid "be62cf62-6aea-5012-9408-1100a5054418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501848} {:answer/id #uuid "d9fde4db-e8cf-5651-9156-5164910baced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501901} {:answer/id #uuid "dac05560-604f-59a2-a9d8-9b854a529250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9fde4db-e8cf-5651-9156-5164910baced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501901} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7557141-90d9-599b-a963-7c24580db883" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26e491f3-a709-5e27-8211-3cc9fc3f4884" :question/math-academy-id "q-130555" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c827895f55c7615d8c74b4cc2b25dabaa674108a5889ca78656d812b3a09216.png)\n\nThe function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Calculate $f(-3)+f(2).$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the necessary function values using the graph.\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- When $x=-3,$ we have $y=-2.$ So, $f(-3)=-2.$\n\n- When $x=2,$ the function is undefined.\n\nTherefore, since $f(2)$ is undefined, the sum $f(-3)+f(2)$ is undefined too."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55343d43-c173-5ba8-9a0e-58a76a282b7f" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f92356c-4590-5619-8068-2113ec753adc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8c83aacabad16e7c0ca61b1e63a952222d0f3c32c5b11b1ec8045476b7888dde.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501520} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9fc9fc-b7f0-5888-9c1c-0776092763f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4b5c2e46a4918c1738024b866a059749f257a0053dc15a6b2542d95d520be73e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501697} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e31324-3dd3-5a5d-a012-929cbb3f703b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7eaca86e8ea70870f0242915c3ee9bd0bd6bcc5f0c3647c2cf6f427e8f1a3639.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501773} {:answer/id #uuid "b023b88f-af67-5b9d-a4dc-8ae6688ded59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f3/f39cd25d7aafdf95a066f27503b95906d0cf988e69f3e662409139b629e3e206.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501817} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb611dc-7fa3-51ce-afc5-0416e9d8adac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a2/a278ea47247ddfb2f3ed6e646a416a665cffdd9005db3945d2b834b4e9307079.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbb611dc-7fa3-51ce-afc5-0416e9d8adac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a2/a278ea47247ddfb2f3ed6e646a416a665cffdd9005db3945d2b834b4e9307079.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501840} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e31995-1049-503e-a9fc-864c3641cf3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c840c5f3-0fd7-561e-88b0-406ba1f39b38" :question/math-academy-id "q-130593" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x),$ where $f(x)=3x-3?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of $y=3x-3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=3x-3.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=3(0)-3=-3,$ which gives the point $(0,-3),$ and\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=3(1)-3=0,$ which gives the point $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/58/58731786b9a03b6b76a0a9fa0b91563d2b4a098c8a0e8b1d5ace5690d3ea2913.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502144 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184202 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55343d43-c173-5ba8-9a0e-58a76a282b7f" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15591a5d-1835-55fe-a9ef-2b3a42fa834e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/489be3487923ded92048e0f30ac063239c4e9e051161d50060d977b56da27870.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501507} {:answer/id #uuid "5d839673-73cf-5aae-b908-32dbf36720e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/0188d7a6ac35bd02d97d90bd1bb118f366cdc545645b97cfdc9317725d656eba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501639} {:answer/id #uuid "a20e6777-8d36-51df-8437-6ec22093bf9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ef/ef5d22b7db6730b6717635d4faae7b3c76ed38c1c0a4106f516d8bf151270c73.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501775} {:answer/id #uuid "abc31bdd-9bc6-514c-bbf8-870c2e67c104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/96e8b7c8f04f37e1312c583ede9eccf80bf507f516f10c1286869d49b8894d1e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501807} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3542ec-62a2-5a86-bea3-8b98ffbfd3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/12535d53412daaa23e6318d69f93cf0ee8789450739ad021faf55f99431a18fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d839673-73cf-5aae-b908-32dbf36720e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/01/0188d7a6ac35bd02d97d90bd1bb118f366cdc545645b97cfdc9317725d656eba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501639} :answer-field/id #uuid "adec00fc-9488-5266-85d4-ebcd586ff2c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca565001-9394-5e0a-81d1-cb1231ca5afa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130598" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(x),$ where $f(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}(0)=2,$ which gives the point $(0,2),$ and\n\n- if $x=2$ then $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}(2)=1,$ which gives the point $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](images/14/14ca58b7ec006bba9465672bb00da30e2539125f66a460ad5b5ea38521a836f5.png)"}]}
